WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Retrieval Augmented Generation, or RAG, is the single most useful pattern for building LLM applications.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.200
The problem it solves is simple. Language models don't know your private data and they hallucinate when unsure.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:19.600
RAG fixes this by fetching relevant documents at query time and stuffing them into the model's context window.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.800
The pipeline has two phases. Ingestion: chunk your documents, embed them, and store the vectors in an index.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:34.100
Then at query time you embed the user's question, retrieve the top k chunks, and generate an answer with citations.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:41.500
Chunking strategy matters more than people think. Fixed size chunks are easy, but semantic chunking often wins.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:48.900
For embeddings, start with a well-supported model like text embedding 3 small, or an open source alternative.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:56.200
Vector databases like Pinecone, Weaviate, and Qdrant handle approximate nearest neighbor search at scale.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:03.400
Hybrid search combining dense vectors with BM25 keyword matching gives a big quality boost on rare terms.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:10.700
A reranker model on top of retrieval can lift precision substantially for a small latency cost.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.900
How do you know if your RAG system is good? Evaluate retrieval separately from generation.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:25.300
Retrieval metrics include recall at k and precision at k. Generation is scored with faithfulness and relevance.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:32.000
Build a golden dataset of questions with verified answers before you start tuning anything else.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.900
In future videos we'll build a full RAG pipeline with evaluation. Subscribe so you don't miss it.
